WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.091
Choose the function
Bound the natural logarithm of 1.1 without a calculator. Use the logarithm
function on the interval from one to 1.1.

2
00:00:12.091 --> 00:00:26.455
Check the assumptions
The function is continuous on this interval and differentiable inside it
because every input is positive. All assumptions of the mean value theorem
therefore hold.

3
00:00:26.455 --> 00:00:41.273
Bound the derivative
On this interval, the derivative lies between ten elevenths and one. The lower
bound comes from the largest denominator and the upper bound from the
smallest.

4
00:00:41.273 --> 00:00:56.545
Apply the mean value theorem
The theorem expresses the difference of logarithms as the derivative at an
interior point times the interval's width. Here the width is one tenth, which
is positive.

5
00:00:56.545 --> 00:01:09.091
Obtain the bounds
Multiply the derivative bounds by one tenth without changing the inequality
directions. Then use the fact that ln one equals zero.

6
00:01:09.091 --> 00:01:23.000
Interpret the result
We have obtained rigorous bounds from the theorem. We did not need the exact
interior point, because the derivative bounds were sufficient to finish.
